// Expected: a0 at one session and at two.
//
// Replacing hua_m makes Bob hash it before he checks it, so by the time his tag
// check fails he has already computed on the attacker's value. The question this
// model settles is whether that first hash counts as Bob having accepted hua_m.
//
// It does not. Bob went on to reject the message, and a recipient that halts is
// not credited with accepting a value it merely computed on the way to failing.
// The engine enforces that by abandoning the whole usage scan once the halted
// state is missing a slot that mentions the constant, which is why hua_later has
// to be there: it is the use Bob never reaches.
//
// The rule is strict on purpose, and it was measured the other way. Counting a
// reached use beside an unreached one turns four verdicts the corpus pins into
// false attacks: the replay in concat_split_replay.vp, the third query of both
// spore_ns_pk.vp and spore_nsl_pk.vp, which is exactly where Lowe's fix is
// supposed to show, and the unbuildable substitution in piknik.vp. Whoever
// relaxes this owes those four an answer first.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private hua_k
	generates hua_m
	hua_tag = MAC(hua_k, hua_m)
]

principal Bob[
	knows private hua_k
]

Alice -> Bob: hua_m, [hua_tag]

principal Bob[
	hua_use = HASH(hua_m)
	_ = ASSERT(hua_tag, MAC(hua_k, hua_m))?
	hua_later = HASH(hua_m)
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: hua_m
]
